{"cards": [{"front": "What does the numerator tell us in a fraction?", "back": "The numerator is the top number. It tells us how many equal parts are shaded or taken."}, {"front": "What does the denominator tell us in a fraction?", "back": "The denominator is the bottom number. It tells us how many equal parts the whole is cut into."}, {"front": "Are 1/2 and 2/4 equivalent fractions? Why?", "back": "Yes. Both show the same amount shaded. If you cut each half of 1/2 into 2 pieces, you get 2/4."}, {"front": "To make 1/3 equivalent with denominator 6, what fraction do we get?", "back": "2/6. We multiply both the numerator and denominator by 2. The bar stays the same size, just cut into more pieces."}, {"front": "If you change 2/5 to have denominator 10, what fraction do you write?", "back": "4/10. Both numerator and denominator are multiplied by 2. This means 2/5 and 4/10 are equivalent."}, {"front": "Look at two bar models: one shows 1/2 shaded, the other shows 1/4 shaded. Both bars are the same size. Which is bigger?", "back": "1/2 is bigger. Even though the bottom numbers are different, when the bars are the same size, 1/2 covers more space than 1/4."}, {"front": "What is a common mistake when making equivalent fractions?", "back": "Changing only the top number, not the bottom. For example, changing 1/2 to 2/2 instead of 2/4. Always change both."}, {"front": "Compare 1/8 and 1/3 using bar models of the same size. Which is bigger?", "back": "1/3 is bigger. When the bar is the same size and we take 1 piece from each, the piece in 1/3 is larger because 3 pieces is fewer than 8 pieces."}, {"front": "Put these fractions in order from smallest to biggest: 1/2, 1/4, 1/8. Use bar models of the same size.", "back": "1/8, 1/4, 1/2. As the denominator grows, each piece gets smaller, so the fraction gets smaller."}, {"front": "Are 3/4 and 6/8 equivalent? How do you know?", "back": "Yes. If you shade 3 out of 4 equal parts and 6 out of 8 equal parts on same-size bars, both look the same. 3/4 = 6/8."}, {"front": "Compare 2/3 and 2/5 using bar models of the same size. Which is bigger?", "back": "2/3 is bigger. The numerator is the same in both. When you take 2 pieces from 3 total pieces, you get more than when you take 2 pieces from 5 pieces."}, {"front": "What does it mean for two bar models to represent equivalent fractions?", "back": "It means they show the same amount shaded, even though the bars are divided into different numbers of pieces. The shaded area looks the same."}, {"front": "Put these fractions in order from biggest to smallest: 3/8, 3/5, 3/2. Use bar models of the same size.", "back": "3/2, 3/5, 3/8. The numerator is the same. Bigger denominator means smaller pieces, so the fraction is smaller."}, {"front": "Why is it important to draw both bar models the same physical size when comparing fractions?", "back": "Because it helps you see which shaded area is actually bigger. If the bars are different sizes, it is hard to compare fairly."}]}
